A1. We are starting out with this, as good housekeepers do in order to make both ends meet. ~ BUDGET
A7. Fools—Dumbells—Blockheads—Idiots—Now it is up to you to call these poor fish another name. ~ SAPS
A11. Something appetizing for the hot season. ~ PAPRIKA
A12. This one has supported a poor crippled soldier since the war. ~ CRUTCH
A15. At four o'clock when the milk-man comes, this is the termination of slumber. ~ ER
A16. This one is truly Scotch. ~ ANE
A17. When you do this it will certainly bring out all the relations. (This is not reading a will or owning a summer cottage.) ~ COMPARE
A18. Have your eraser handy because anything here will be incorrect. ~ WRONG
A20. This doesn't carry much weight with Gandhi's followers. ~ SER
A21. Don't have too many of these in the fire. ~ IRONS
A23. The original singer for the bath-tub. ~ TEAKETTLE
A25. At last we have found a sucker that is willing to park in this space. ~ LEECH
A26. This fellow has a lodgical mind. ~ ELK
A27. These are always running around the deck trying to keep people from falling overboard. ~ RAILS
A28. Some wet stuff that you can always get in the A & P. ~ SEA
A29. These two characters always come before tea. (Do you think they will stand for rhubarb sauce?) ~ RS
A30. Here is just the ticket. (plur.) ~ FARES
A31. What your old man was when he went to court. ~ BEAU
A34. Any fisherman will tell you that these are easily caught in damp weather. ~ COLDS
A35. These have always been a shifty lot. ~ GEARS
A37. An obsolete word. ~ OBEY
A38. A blow-out that was extremely shocking. ~ BLAST
A39. This stands very high in transportation circles. ~ EL
A41. The name of a German electrician. ~ OHM
A43. Do not put your faith in this because it is not on the level. ~ SLANT
A44. Cal's rival. ~ FLA
A45. A sugar producer. ~ MAPLE
A48. Bawl rooms. ~ NURSERIES
A50. This girl married a cockney named Blazes. ~ ELLEN
A51. A metal container. ~ ORE
A52. A large stockholder. ~ RANCH
A53. High pressure peanut salesmen. ~ VENDERS
A55. This one is always getting in some mix-up at the camp. ~ ANT
A56. It might lead you into temptation. ~ TE
A57. The only way to get money from a Scotchman. ~ EXTORT
A58. These have been entirely cut out and are seldom seen at formal society affairs. ~ SLEEVES
A60. What any Chicago school teacher does for her vacation ~ OWES
A61. Assembled all the stock before a run on the bank. ~ HERDED

D1. Double-headers. ~ BARRELS
D2. What she called him on the phone. ~ UP
D3. Imbibed. ~ DRANK
D4. This has helped mix many highballs and yet it is considered extra dry. ~ GINGERALE
D5. To make a silver dollar roll a little farther. ~ EKE
D6. Another one like this and it will be "good-night" for you. ~ TA
D7. Some one deserves a lot of honors for these additions to bridge. ~ SCORES
D8. Keep this one up your sleeve. ~ ARM
D9. In the eyes of the professor this one is dilatory. ~ PUPIL
D10. What the star gazers do out in Hollywood. ~ STARE
D11. One of the first Yankee pitchers. ~ PEWTER
D13. This lucky old Greek probably never had to pay an income tax—no wonder he was rich. ~ CROESUS
D14. From this time. ~ HENCE
D17. You might lease one of these apartments from the judge. ~ CELLS
D19. Believe it or not, this one started out as a nut. ~ OAK
D20. You hear a lot of grunting on the farm about these. ~ STIES
D22. The rock of Ireland. ~ SHAM
D24. This one is always behindhand. ~ TARDY
D30. There used to be a charm in connection with this. ~ FOB
D31. Some of these came from Boston, while others are from Lima, Ohio. ~ BEANS
D32. An Oriental. ~ EASTERNER
D33. What John Barrymore puts into his roles. ~ ART
D34. What an eccentric person usually has. ~ COMPLEX
D35. A silent reproach from your partner. ~ GLARE
D36. Where James must go. ~ HOME
D38. These have a clouded reputation. ~ BLURS
D39. If the Democratic candidate is this he wiil satisfy his party all right. ~ ELECTED
D40. Can you blame these for being so attached to her beautiful dark eyes? ~ LASHES
D42. To make it 50-50. ~ HALVE
D43. A beastly broadcast (plur.). ~ SNORTS
D44. A swimmer's guide. ~ FIN
D46. Slow music. ~ LENTO
D47. What a man must do with his worldly goods. (Marriage ceremony.) ~ ENDOW
D49. Put him in his proper place. ~ RATED
D54. As we have said before. ~ ERE
D55. In England they use this to wet their appetites. ~ ALE
D58. An anti-racket expression. ~ SH
D59. Pronoun 1st person plural—Swedish. ~ VE


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A7: Fools—Dumbells—Blockheads—Idiots—Now it is up to you to call these poor fish another name. → Dumbbells
D39: If the Democratic candidate is this he wiil satisfy his party all right. → will

